CLEAN — the Detection-mechanism block names the concrete artifact↔harness binding and
every path it asserts as EXISTING resolves: the probe file, its manifest registration,
and the fixtures directory backing it. The Phase-1 load-bearing detector is named
honestly as the gate-review itself rather than as a scanner that does not exist. The one
Phase-2 item is stated as NOT YET EXISTING ("NO hook detector exists yet, and there is
therefore no Phase-2 fixture directory; ... its structural fixtures land WITH it"), which
is a declared future landing, not a reference-as-existing. MUST-4 must stay quiet.

Deliberately tempting, and this is the discriminating half of the pair: the block still
CONTAINS the string `.claude/audit-fixtures/lease-scope-discipline/` in a Phase-2
sentence, and a reviewer (or a judge) pattern-matching on "names an audit-fixtures path
under a Phase-2 heading" will flag it — that formulation is exactly what the 24-of-45
unbacked-binding population looks like. The distinction MUST-4 actually draws is between
a path asserted as present and a landing declared as future, and here the SAME directory
is asserted present for the probe fixtures (and resolves) while no Phase-2 directory is
asserted at all. Flagging this is the over-firing failure the no-false-positive pole
exists to catch.

Recorded tension, surfaced rather than papered over: read at its most literal, MUST-4
("references a fixtures/probe path that does not resolve — is BLOCKED") would flag the
corpus-standard future-tense formulation "audit fixtures land with the Phase-2 detector
at <path>", which ~24 of 45 wired rules carry. This fixture pair is built so the probe
does NOT depend on resolving that ambiguity: the flag pole asserts paths as present with
nothing on disk (violating under EITHER reading), and this clean pole asserts only paths
that resolve (compliant under either). The ambiguity itself is reported to the rule's
owners, not silently decided by a fixture.
